  <a href="/images/docs-terrain-colors.png"><img src="/images/docs-terrain-colors.png" alt="Biome Rules settings in the native editor, showing the default biome, Hex RGBA color, precision controls and biome properties." width="5120" height="2806" loading="lazy"></a>
  <figcaption>The Biome Rules node opens the shared appearance settings. Change a color, then Generate to update the terrain preview. Click to view the screenshot at full size.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Open the example directly in this view:</p>
<pre><code>./build/linux-release/demi-editor --project examples/terrain_graph_3d --terrain-graph
./build/linux-release/demi-editor --project examples/terrain_graph_3d --terrain-settings-node biome_rules</code></pre>
<p>The graph toolbar uses action icons with descriptive tooltips and your current shortcut bindings. Ctrl-click or drag a selection box to select several nodes. Copy/paste and duplicate retain their parameters and internal links while assigning fresh IDs. Cut, paste, multi-delete and multi-node movement each form one graph-draft Undo step. Shortcuts act on the focused graph; text fields keep normal editing. Configure mappings and alternatives in <strong>Edit → Editor Settings → Keyboard shortcuts</strong>. See <a href="/docs/editor-workspace">workspace shortcuts</a>.</p>
<p>The canvas uses the whole Terrain Graph window width. Recipe settings float over it; collapse their header to leave only the title strip, or use <strong>Hide settings</strong> to remove them entirely. Scrolling over settings scrolls that panel without zooming the graph. <strong>Show local modules</strong> opens an optional local palette; the searchable <strong>Terrain Nodes</strong> window can be docked separately. Terrain Graph itself can sit beside Viewport so you can inspect the generated terrain while editing its graph. Node footers show the last generation's CPU time or whether its result was reused.</p>

<h2>Source assets, caching and shipping</h2>
<p>The editable source is a <code>*.terrain.json</code> document with <code>format_version</code>, a stable <code>asset://</code> ID and a <code>recipe</code>. Its ordinary <code>*.asset.json</code> manifest uses type <code>Terrain</code> and importer <code>terrain_heightfield</code>. Generated previews live in the platform cache directory, not in the developer's asset folder.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "id": "landscape",
  "components": {
    "Transform3D": { "position": [0, 0, 0] },
    "Terrain3D": { "asset": "asset://terrain/landscape" }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Several scene entities can reference the same Terrain asset while using different placement transforms. The recipe remains shared. Editing the asset does not add an override recipe to each scene instance.</p>
<p>Cook and package builds produce a <code>*.terrain.bin</code> payload and rewrite the cooked asset manifest to reference it. The payload includes the prepared surface, edit checkpoint, biome data, exclusions, water data and scatter placements. Runtime loading decodes that data and creates native render/collision resources; it does not evaluate noise, erosion, water carving or scatter selection. Missing or corrupt payloads produce an error rather than silently generating replacement terrain.</p>
<pre><code>demi cook --project examples/terrain_graph_3d \
  --output build/terrain-graph-cooked --platform linux
demi run --project build/terrain-graph-cooked</code></pre>
<p>Development tools can prepare missing or stale source-asset caches before running a project. Warm source loads reuse prepared results; normal shipped projects load their binary payloads. Source edits and dependency changes invalidate preparation through the shared asset/cook services.</p>
<p><code>Terrain3D.lod_error_pixels</code> sets the maximum projected surface error for render detail selection. It defaults to 2 pixels; smaller values retain more detail, and 0 disables render LOD. The value must be finite and nonnegative. Each camera reads the terrain owner's current value. Changing this setting preserves the generated heightfield and collision geometry; it does not change terrain residency or gameplay scatter. The Terrain Inspector exposes the same setting.</p>
<p>The allowance uses the originating camera's presentation viewport pixels. Shadow views use that camera's detail decision, and reduced render resolution does not loosen the allowance. Rough, sparsely sampled terrain may stay at full detail even at a distance. LOD does not extend the camera's <code>far_clip</code>: choose a far plane that covers the landscape you want to see. GPU mesh eviction only retires unused render resources; it does not remove nearby terrain or its collision.</p>
<p>On supported devices, compatible opaque terrain uses shared grid patches. Vertex shaders sample height and normal textures, and compatible patches are submitted in instanced batches. Biome and surface-role maps select neighboring material-palette entries per pixel. Supported solid materials blend across each heightfield cell: base colors interpolate in linear color space, together with metallic, roughness and lit/unlit contributions. This reduces mesh uploads and draw submissions without requiring a lower terrain resolution or a looser LOD setting. Mixed one-sided and two-sided regions preserve their face rules through palette flags.</p>
<p>GPU material boundaries now interpolate the four neighboring sample materials instead of using a hard per-sample lookup. Material IDs are decoded separately before their properties blend; IDs and face-culling flags are never interpolated. This is spatial blending over one grid cell, not height-map-aware layering or textured PBR terrain. The ordinary mesh fallback still uses discrete triangle-majority material assignment, so boundaries can look different while GPU resources are preparing or when fallback is required. Custom shaders, textured or transparent terrain materials, unsupported device capabilities and exhausted budgets use the ordinary mesh path; the runtime reports the reason instead of silently substituting a material.</p>
<p>Field texture data and compatibility-path reduced meshes are prepared on worker threads. Until resources are ready, full-detail meshes remain visible. <code>Environment3D.terrain_lod_cache_mb</code> sets the reduced-mesh CPU payload budget and the separate GPU-field staging allowance in MiB (default 64). <code>terrain_lod_prepare_jobs</code> limits each preparation queue's outstanding and newly started jobs per presentation (default 2); zero pauses new preparation while valid ready resources remain usable. These are per-renderer queue/cache budgets, not a combined process-memory limit. Shared heightfields, full-detail meshes, collision and allocator overhead are outside the tracked payload budget.</p>
<p><code>Environment3D.terrain_gpu_cache_mb</code> independently budgets GPU terrain field textures and shared patch buffers in MiB (default 128). Zero disables GPU patches and selects ordinary mesh rendering. Unchanged height/normal channels can be shared across appearance edits; changed channels are transactionally replaced. Field-size or palette limits follow actual device texture capabilities and fall back without imposing a smaller authored terrain limit. Source heightfields, picking and full-detail collision remain authoritative.</p>
<p>For a deliberately procedural game, an entity can instead specify <code>Terrain3D.recipe</code> inline. That explicitly requests runtime generation. <code>asset</code> and <code>recipe</code> cannot be supplied together.</p>

<h2>Landforms and biomes</h2>
<p>A landform is the shape of the ground. A biome is how that ground looks. They are authored separately, so recolouring a surface never changes the terrain and reshaping a hill never changes its tint.</p>
<p>A <code>landforms</code> object is keyed by name. Each shape uses <code>base_height</code>, <code>height_variation</code>, <code>feature_size</code>, <code>roughness</code> and <code>octaves</code>, with optional noise-profile and coordinate controls below. The recipe selects its fallback shape with <code>default_landform</code>. The Noise terrain graph node exposes the same controls for its own source; a Landform node reads the recipe's named shapes.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>profile</code></td><td><code>fbm</code></td><td><code>fbm</code> produces smooth fractal relief; <code>ridged</code> produces sharper ridge structures.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>warp_amplitude</code></td><td>0</td><td>Displaces sampling coordinates by this distance in terrain-local world units. Zero disables domain warp.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>warp_feature_size</code></td><td>128</td><td>Width of the coordinate-warp features in terrain-local world units; must be positive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>stretch</code></td><td>1</td><td>Elongates features along the heading. Values above one stretch them; the value must be positive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>heading</code></td><td>0</td><td>Angle from terrain-local +X toward +Z, in degrees. At zero, stretch acts along X.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Domain warp runs before heading and stretch. These controls affect the shape, not its biome tint or material. Values must be finite; warp amplitude is nonnegative. Unsupported combinations of scale, warp and octave count report a sampling-domain diagnostic rather than producing invalid heights. Omitting the new controls preserves the existing FBm output; new controls at their defaults are omitted from canonical landform JSON.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "default_landform": "mountains",
  "landforms": {
    "mountains": {
      "profile": "ridged",
      "height_variation": 450,
      "feature_size": 650,
      "roughness": 0.35,
      "octaves": 4,
      "warp_amplitude": 100,
      "warp_feature_size": 500,
      "stretch": 2,
      "heading": 30
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>A <code>biomes</code> object is keyed by name and each entry takes a <code>color</code>, an optional ordinary <code>material</code> asset, a <code>texture_scale</code>, and an optional <code>landform</code> naming the shape its ground uses. Leave <code>landform</code> out to use the recipe's default. Several biomes can share one landform, and one landform can serve several looks, so a shared rocky shape costs nothing to reuse.</p>
<p>The two are edited in the Terrain Graph's generation settings: <strong>Landforms</strong> for elevation and relief, <strong>Biomes</strong> for tint and landform choice.</p>

<h3>Textures, scenery and water: current support</h3>
<figure class="docs-figure">
  <a href="/images/docs-terrain-material-binding.png"><img src="/images/docs-terrain-material-binding.png" alt="Native terrain viewport showing an ordinary material's checker texture repeated continuously across sixteen generated chunks." width="5120" height="2806" loading="lazy"></a>
  <figcaption>A checker material tiled across terrain chunks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Biome assignment creates discrete triangle groups that now use the biome's ordinary <code>Material</code> asset. In Terrain Graph settings, open a biome's properties and choose <strong>Render material (Material)</strong>. Its base-colour/albedo texture and supported metallic/roughness controls use the shared Mesh Renderer path in preview, Game View and cooked runtime. Biome <code>color</code> is still a tint: use <code>[1,1,1,1]</code> when you want the material's appearance without colour tinting.</p>
<p><code>texture_scale</code> is positive finite repetitions per terrain-local unit and defaults to 1. A value of 0.5 repeats a texture every two units; 2 repeats twice per unit. Coordinates are continuous across chunk boundaries. Set the Texture2D asset's Wrap to Repeat for tiling; a clamped texture will not repeat. Scaling the terrain entity scales this authored texture pattern with it. Reset removes the scale override.</p>
<p>Material and tint changes preserve terrain geometry and collision; texture-scale changes update UVs and GPU geometry revision without rebuilding heights or colliders. Generate applies the draft through the existing update/history path, and prepared/cooked payloads retain material IDs and scales without needing the source recipe at runtime.</p>
<p>Prepared binary terrain is read-only in the editor. Open the source project to edit its graph or use terrain brushes, then cook the project again.</p>
<p>Typed <code>terrain_material</code> and <code>terrain_material_set</code> data assets remain authoring definitions, not renderable biome materials. Validation warns when one is assigned here. Smooth blended layers, triplanar mapping and general PBR texture maps remain pending; this binding step is not the complete high-quality landscape renderer.</p>
<p>Scatter Palette places models or prefabs on the generated surface. Those objects use their own Mesh Renderer materials. Connect Biome Rules before Scatter Palette when biome rules should control placement.</p>
<p>Water Body nodes carve finite lakes, oceans or river paths and produce surface/depth data that survives cooking. Connect the water result to Terrain Output: Generate publishes transparent, directly lit water meshes alongside the terrain, and cooked games load the same surfaces without carving again. Water follows the terrain placement and enabled state. It has no solid collider, so it does not act as a walkable floor.</p>
<p>Water Body nodes expose <strong>Shallow RGBA</strong>, <strong>Deep RGBA</strong>, <strong>Absorption distance</strong> and <strong>Roughness</strong>. The alpha channels control shallow and deep opacity. Absorption distance is positive, in terrain-local units: at that depth the shallow contribution falls to about 37%. The mesh renderer interpolates depth-derived vertex colours and opacity; this is an artistic depth approximation, not scene refraction or a fluid simulation. Generated water meshes are native runtime resources, not authored entities or extra asset files.</p>
<p>Shore triangles are clipped where the terrain meets the water plane rather than extending a full grid cell beyond it. Queries use the same terrain-triangle interpolation independently of the visible mesh. A bounded lake still needs banks at or above its chosen level: the engine warns after Generate if wet ground reaches the authored boundary below that level. It does not silently lower your level, raise banks or simulate spilling. Lower the water, enlarge the boundary, or sculpt a containing basin.</p>
<p>Press Generate after changing water appearance or geometry. Shore contours follow the terrain's resolution. Animated waves, foam, reflection/refraction, underwater camera effects and buoyancy/swimming integration are not currently supported.</p>
<figure class="docs-figure">
  <a href="/images/docs-terrain-water-publication.png"><img src="/images/docs-terrain-water-publication.png" alt="Native terrain viewport with a transparent blue water surface over the generated lake basin." width="5120" height="2806" loading="lazy"></a>
  <figcaption>A lake in a sculpted basin, with clipped shores and depth-derived colour/opacity. Reflections, waves and foam are still pending.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Existing recipes that put <code>base_height</code> and friends inside a biome are rejected with a message naming the field and telling you to move it to a landform. They are not silently ignored, because a dropped height field would quietly flatten terrain that used to have relief.</p>
<p>Without a graph, a painted biome can also select its landform and change height. With a graph, painting overrides biome labels without replacing the graph's generated shape; use sculpting or graph modules to change height.</p>
<p>A recipe with no rules behaves as it did before rules existed. A biome referenced by the default setting, a rule or a painted region cannot be removed until those references are removed, and a biome naming a landform that does not exist is rejected.</p>

<h2>Query water from Lua</h2>
<p>Import <code>local Water = require("demi.terrain.water")</code> to query prepared water data. <code>Water.sample(position, terrain_id)</code> takes a world-space <code>Vec3</code>: a dense, one-based numeric array of exactly three finite numbers, <code>{x, y, z}</code>. The optional <code>terrain_id</code> is the stable entity ID of a terrain placement; omit it to query loaded, enabled terrains. Dry or unavailable water returns <code>nil</code>. A wet column can return a sample even when the point is above its surface or below its bed; check <code>underwater</code> for immersion.</p>
<p>A <code>WaterSample</code> contains:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>terrain_id: string</code> and <code>body_id: string</code> identify the terrain placement and its water body.</li>
  <li><code>kind: "lake" | "ocean" | "river"</code> identifies the body kind.</li>
  <li><code>surface: Vec3</code> is a world-space surface point, and <code>normal: Vec3</code> is a world-space unit surface normal.</li>
  <li><code>depth: number</code> is the bed-to-surface depth in world units along terrain-local up.</li>
  <li><code>underwater: boolean</code> is true when the queried point lies at or above the bed and below the surface. Points on the surface or below the bed are not underwater.</li>
</ul>
<p>Queries follow translated, rotated and scaled terrain placements. The surface point projects the query along terrain-local up, which is not necessarily global vertical on tilted terrain. Sampling has no renderer dependency and does not generate terrain on demand.</p>

<h3>Track point immersion</h3>
<p><code>Water.tracker(terrain_id)</code> creates an opaque <code>WaterTracker</code> with the same optional terrain filter. Call <code>tracker:update(position)</code> with a world-space <code>Vec3</code> to receive an ordered array of <code>WaterEvent</code> values. Each event has <code>phase: "enter" | "exit" | "stay"</code> and <code>sample: WaterSample</code>. The tracker follows one point, not collider extents.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The first update inside water emits <code>enter</code>.</li>
  <li>Remaining inside the same body emits <code>stay</code> on every explicit update.</li>
  <li>Switching bodies emits <code>exit</code> for the old body, followed by <code>enter</code> for the new body.</li>
  <li>Moving outside emits <code>exit</code> once; subsequent outside updates return an empty array.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>tracker:current()</code> returns the immersed sample retained by the latest update, or <code>nil</code>. <code>tracker:reset()</code> clears that state without an event. Terrain unload, disable and regeneration are reflected on the next update. Tracker identity includes the terrain placement, body and scene, so duplicate body IDs across placements or scenes do not mix. Scene identity remains private and is not returned in samples.</p>
<p>Call <code>update</code> from <code>on_fixed_update</code> or <code>on_update</code> to choose the cadence and implement gameplay mechanics. Events are returned to that call; there are no interrupt callbacks or automatic global listeners. <code>Transform3D.get_world_position</code> resolves parent transforms for the tracked entity:</p>
<pre><code class="language-lua">local Water = require("demi.terrain.water")
local Transform3D = require("demi.transform3d")

local script = {}

function script:on_create()
    self.water_tracker = Water.tracker()
end

function script:on_fixed_update(dt)
    local x, y, z = Transform3D.get_world_position(self.entity_id)
    if x == nil or y == nil or z == nil then
        self.water_tracker:reset()
        return
    end

    local position = {x, y, z}
    for _, event in ipairs(self.water_tracker:update(position)) do
        -- Handle enter, exit or stay here; event.sample describes the body.
        self.last_water_phase = event.phase
    end
    self.underwater = self.water_tracker:current() ~= nil
end

return script</code></pre>
<p>Use <code>Water.sample(position)</code> for a single query without tracking transitions. Animated waves and automatic buoyancy are not implemented; these queries provide point immersion and depth for script-controlled behavior.</p>
<p>The graph example includes an optional <strong>Water Sensor</strong> script component in <code>scripts/water_sensor.lua</code>. Attach it to an entity to emit <code>water_enter</code>, <code>water_exit</code> and <code>water_stay</code> through <code>demi.events</code>, with <code>entity_id</code> and <code>water</code> in the payload. Configure <code>offset_y</code> for the sampled point and disable <code>emit_stay</code> when only transitions are needed.</p>

<h2>Manual adjustments</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Raise / Lower:</strong> adds or subtracts height, weighted by the brush. Use a low strength for small adjustments.</li>
  <li><strong>Flatten:</strong> captures the height where the stroke starts and moves affected samples toward that elevation.</li>
  <li><strong>Smooth:</strong> averages neighbouring samples using the pre-edit surface for each stroke stamp.</li>
  <li><strong>Protect:</strong> records absolute sample heights and keeps them through later regeneration and sculpting. Protection snapshots use the original grid.</li>
  <li><strong>Paint exclusion:</strong> paints a separate 0&ndash;1 mask over the surface without changing any height. Choose <strong>Exclude</strong> to add mask weight or <strong>Erase</strong> to clear a subsection. Later generation stages use the mask to skip an area; the editor can preview it directly. Enable <strong>Preview exclusions</strong> to see it in the viewport.</li>
</ul>
<p>The brush outline follows the actual surface. Release a drag to generate and apply the stroke as an undoable edit; Esc cancels the active stroke. Generate or discard pending settings before painting. Regeneration changes the procedural base and replays the stored edits; it does not discard them.</p>
<p>Buildings and other scene objects remain independent and are never deleted or moved by regeneration. Terrain changes may leave objects above or below the ground; repositioning them is an explicit editing action.</p>

<h2>Layers</h2>
<p>The recipe stores five named, toggleable layers by default: <strong>generation</strong>, <strong>biomes</strong>, <strong>sculpt</strong>, <strong>protection</strong> and <strong>exclusions</strong>. Each layer has a stable ID, a display name and an enabled toggle in the Terrain Graph's settings. The component Inspector selects which eligible layer receives a brush stroke.</p>
<p>Disabling a layer removes its strokes from evaluation without deleting them, so a stroke can be switched off and restored while you iterate. Strokes on disabled layers keep their authored data.</p>
<p>Two ordering rules matter:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Base stages always evaluate before surface edits, regardless of the order layers appear in the recipe. Disabling the <strong>generation</strong> layer therefore freezes the base heights and lets you sculpt on top of them.</li>
  <li>Within a stage, layers and the strokes inside them keep their authored order. Later strokes in the same stage paint over earlier ones.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can add extra layers to the biome, sculpt, protection and exclusion groups, and rename any layer. The <strong>generation</strong> layer is the base noise pass and cannot be removed. Layer names and toggles are draft settings: press Generate to apply them.</p>
<p>Every brush except Select targets a layer, chosen in the <strong>Target layer</strong> field of the viewport brush controls. The list only offers layers of the kind the brush writes to, and disabled layers cannot be selected. Flatten keeps a captured target height and raise/lower keeps a separate additive offset, so the two kinds of manual adjustment stay distinct when you retune strength and falloff.</p>

<h2>What a brush stroke changes</h2>
<p>Brush data stores shared parameters once and ordered stamp positions in a <code>points</code> list. A single radial operation can use <code>center</code>. Consecutive stamps with identical settings share one entry; different settings or protection snapshots remain separate. Repeated positions and overlapping stamps are retained because they affect brush strength. This changes the source representation, not the terrain result.</p>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "type": "flatten",
  "target_height": 12,
  "radius": 8,
  "points": [[24, 32], [25.6, 32], [27.2, 32]]
}</code></pre>
<p>New viewport strokes use this representation automatically. For existing data, choose <strong>Compact brush data</strong> in the graph's edit settings, then Generate and save the terrain asset. The CLI can preview the same conversion with <code>demi terrain compact path/to/landscape.terrain.json</code>; add <code>--write</code> to save it, then reimport its asset manifest. Compaction preserves unrelated recipe fields and validates native operation equivalence before saving. It does not replace the recipe with baked heights or remove regeneration and layer editing.</p>
<p>Ordinary brush work is incremental. The generator keeps the generated base surface separately from the sculpted result, so a stroke recomputes only the samples the brush actually touched, plus the one-sample border that lighting and neighbouring smoothing depend on. Meshes and collision are rebuilt only for the affected chunks; untouched chunks keep their existing resources and are not re-uploaded.</p>
<p>Full regeneration also limits each sculpt stamp to its brush footprint instead of scanning the whole terrain for that stamp. Brush order, smoothing snapshots and protected samples keep their existing meaning.</p>
<p>A local edit produces the same heights as a full regeneration of the same recipe. History is stored as before/after sample values, so once a stroke is applied, Undo and Redo restore the recipe and the visible surface together. They stay unavailable while a stroke is still being dragged or a preview is pending, so that a half-finished stroke is never recorded as an edit.</p>
<p>Some changes genuinely invalidate the whole base and trigger a full regeneration instead: changing the seed, terrain size, resolution or chunk size, and editing landform generation parameters such as base height, height variation, feature size, roughness, octave count, profile, domain warp or directional stretch. A change to the grid asks how to proceed rather than silently discarding edits, as described under changing terrain dimensions below.</p>
<p>Editing only a biome tint is cheaper again: it recolours the existing triangles without rebuilding heights or colliders. Play mode never starts on a stale terrain surface, because Play waits for pending terrain work to finish, and a published stroke updates the visible mesh and its static collision together.</p>

<h2>Biome rules</h2>
<p>Biome rules assign biomes automatically from the finished surface, so a landscape no longer needs manual painting. Add a <code>rules</code> array to the recipe. Each rule names a target biome and may band the following conditions; an omitted condition is unconstrained.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>elevation</code> &mdash; world height, as a <code>[minimum, maximum]</code> pair.</li>
  <li><code>slope</code> &mdash; surface steepness in degrees, 0 flat to 90 vertical.</li>
  <li><code>moisture</code> &mdash; a stable climate field in <code>[0, 1]</code>, independent of your biome list.</li>
  <li><code>water_distance</code> &mdash; world distance to the nearest water sample.</li>
  <li><code>substrate</code> &mdash; any of <code>soil</code>, <code>rock</code>, <code>sand</code> or <code>wet</code>, derived from the surface rather than painted.</li>
</ul>
<p>On conflict the highest <code>priority</code> wins. An equal priority is broken by blend weight, so a stronger transition outranks a weaker one, and a full tie keeps the earlier rule, so the result never depends on evaluation order. <code>blend</code> is a transition width in world units measured from the lower edge of the rule's <code>elevation</code> band. It softens how strongly a rule claims the ground just above that edge, and that strength is what breaks a <code>priority</code> tie. It does not cross-fade biome colours: surface tint is a discrete biome per cell, so a higher <code>blend</code> gives a rule the edge rather than a gradient. Use overlapping <code>elevation</code> bands with different priorities to shape a transition.</p>
<p>Painted regions always win over rules, so a manual stroke stays authoritative over automatic assignment. Disabling a biome layer removes its rules exactly as it removes its regions. Because rules read the whole surface, changing one rebuilds the terrain base; sculpting alone stays incremental.</p>
<p>With no <code>rules</code> array, the default biome and painted regions determine assignment. Rules are draft settings like every other recipe field: the <strong>Biome rules</strong> section of the Terrain Graph edits them, and they take effect when you press Generate.</p>
<p>In that section each rule exposes its ID, target biome, target layer, priority, blend, a checkbox and minimum/maximum pair per band, and a substrate multi-select. The layer list only offers enabled biome layers; a rule pointing at a disabled one is not evaluated, and the section says so. A band checkbox removes its key from the recipe rather than writing a null, which keeps the saved source free of conventions that mean nothing. Rule IDs are referenced by nothing except the rule itself, so renaming one is safe.</p>
<p>The <strong>Rule mask overlay</strong> selector in the viewport brush controls draws a mask over the surface: <strong>None</strong>, <strong>Biome</strong>, <strong>Elevation</strong> or <strong>Slope</strong>. The overlay samples the committed generated field, so it does not change until you press Generate, even if the draft rules differ. Elevation is normalized against the range the current field actually spans, and slope is read from the committed normals. There is no moisture overlay: moisture is derived in a transient pass over the field during generation and is not stored on the result, so drawing it would either cost a fresh pass every frame or show a fabricated zero.</p>
<p>Biome rules are an experimental authoring aid. Drainage now exposes flow as a graph mask. Biome-rule fields do not yet consume flow or basin membership directly.</p>

<h2>Landscape presets</h2>
<p>Every project has eight built-in starters: <strong>Flat Building Site</strong>, <strong>Desert Dunes</strong>, <strong>Rolling Hills</strong>, <strong>Mountain Range</strong>, <strong>Basin</strong>, <strong>Plateau</strong>, <strong>Coastal Island</strong> and <strong>Drainage River</strong>. They need no imported art. Each uses a 128 × 128 world-unit surface with 64 × 64 cells, biome tints and an editable graph. Comment nodes explain shape, appearance and output. They are learning examples, not simulations of geological dune or mountain formation.</p>
<p>Follow the graph left to right. Change a Noise node's <strong>Noise profile</strong>, <strong>Height variation</strong> and <strong>Feature size</strong> for relief. Domain warp bends the sampling pattern; directional stretch and heading organize elongated features. Biome Rules opens the shared appearance settings. Generate previews changes; <strong>Apply changes to asset</strong> saves a shared terrain. <strong>Undo terrain</strong> in the Presets tab restores an applied landscape; graph-draft edits use Undo graph.</p>
<h3>Drainage River Route</h3>
<p><strong>River Route</strong> (<code>river_route</code>) consumes a Drainage node result, which retains the terrain field that produced it. Choose <code>source_x</code> and <code>source_z</code> in terrain-local world units; the source must lie inside the field and is snapped to the nearest terrain sample. The node follows drainage guidance only along nonascending terrain-triangle edges. It does not choose watershed sources automatically or invent uphill spill routes.</p>
<p><code>channel_width</code> and <code>channel_depth</code> are finite positive world-unit values. The routed bed starts at the traced height minus channel depth. At a receiving water body, a constant bed floor prevents the river surface from falling below the joined surface; this can form a flat backwater reach without introducing an uphill bed. A sea-level endpoint uses the drainage sea level as that receiving surface. <code>water_depth</code> is finite and positive, no greater than channel depth; the water surface follows that bed at this depth. Defaults are source <code>[64,64]</code>, channel width 4, channel depth 2 and water depth 1. The graph node ID becomes the stable water body ID.</p>
<p>Connect River Route's Field and Water outputs to Terrain Output. Its optional Existing water input accepts water that matches the Drainage node's source field. To route into a lake, ocean or another river, generate that body first, connect its Field output to Drainage and its Water output to River Route. The route stops at the first existing wet column and reports the joined body ID. An already-wet source retains existing water without adding a river. Short or rejected routes also retain existing water.</p>
<p>Select the River Route node to read its bounded endpoint status: sea level, field edge or an existing water body. Closed-basin and blocked-surface routes produce warnings and end there; they do not imply a valid sea outlet. A route shorter than two samples, or water that cannot be placed, leaves the input field unchanged and publishes no new river, with a diagnostic explaining why.</p>
<p>Authored Water Body river nodes also expose <code>river_surface_mode</code>: <code>level</code> is the default and uses Water level; <code>follow_bed</code> uses the authored XYZ path's bed elevation plus positive <code>river_depth</code> (default 1). Water level is ignored in Follow bed mode. Terrain-triangle water queries and cooked surfaces use the same resolved per-position surface. This is a prescribed channel and water surface, not a time-dependent flow simulation.</p>
<h3>Drainage and erosion masks</h3>
<p>Drainage exposes a <strong>Flow accumulation</strong> mask alongside its drainage data. Interior samples include all upstream contributions, normalized to the largest interior value; border outlets retain zero mask weight. Erosion exposes <strong>Deposited sediment</strong> and <strong>Eroded material</strong> masks. Each independently maps its largest positive net deposit or cut to 1, with zero where that kind of change is absent. Connect these mask pins to Height Blend or Height Offset to drive later graph stages.</p>
<p>Erosion retains signed height change per sample in its in-memory graph results: positive means net deposition, negative means net removal. This includes hydraulic and thermal changes; it is not a fluid-depth simulation or a new cooked gameplay query. Reapplying sculpt strokes reuses the generated base and does not silently restart global erosion.</p>
<p>Select an Erosion node to read its hydraulic transport totals: eroded, deposited, exported at open field edges and suspended. These are sums in discrete sample-height units, not physical sediment mass or volume. Suspended records unfinished bounded transport for this generation operation; it is not retained fluid state. The Deposition and Cut masks instead describe net height changes, including thermal relaxation, so they are distinct from these gross hydraulic totals. Final graph caches and prepared terrain assets retain the per-node ledger for inspection, including after local sculpt replay.</p>
<h3>Generation quality</h3>
<p>The graph toolbar offers <strong>Preview</strong> and <strong>Final</strong> generation. Final is the default and runs erosion at its authored iteration count, including thermal transport. The Erosion node’s iteration count controls hydraulic transport: zero skips droplets, while thermal strength independently controls Final thermal relaxation. Preview keeps the same grid, source noise and drainage but uses fewer hydraulic erosion iterations and droplets, shorter droplet walks and no thermal pass. A graph without erosion retains the same sampled shape in both modes.</p>
<p>Generation runs in the background with cancellable progress, including progress during erosion. The last completed surface remains visible while work runs. A completed Preview is labeled and shown temporarily; it does not commit the draft recipe or enter final generation caches. Generate Final before Apply, Save, Play or brush editing. <strong>Discard preview</strong> restores the previously applied surface while retaining the graph draft.</p>
<p>Quality is a transient work choice, not an authored terrain field. Source shape caches can be reused across quality modes; erosion and its downstream graph results cannot. Authored sculpt and exclusion strokes run after graph erosion, and ordinary local brush editing reuses that generated base rather than starting another erosion simulation.</p>
<h3>Path Distance Mask</h3>
<p><code>path_distance_mask</code> takes a connected terrain field and outputs a mask around a polyline. Its <code>points</code> are at least two <code>[x,z]</code> pairs in terrain-local world units. The graph editor uses structured point rows with add, remove and reorder controls. Repeated points are valid. Coordinates outside the terrain are also valid, which lets a corridor enter and leave the field.</p>
<p><code>width</code> is the fully weighted <strong>half width</strong>, not the total corridor width. A width of 35 gives a 70-unit core. <code>falloff</code> is the smooth transition beyond that core; zero gives a hard edge. Both are finite and nonnegative. Defaults are points <code>[[0,0],[0,100]]</code>, width 20 and falloff 40. Each coordinate must fit the finite native float range.</p>
<p>For a valley, connect a mountain Noise source and a lower Flat terrain source to Height Blend's A and B inputs, then connect Path Distance Mask to its Mask input. A blend amount of 1 follows the mask completely. The mask changes blend weight; it does not carve or publish water. Use a Water Body node when the corridor also needs a river bed and water data.</p>
<h3>Polygon Mask</h3>
<p><code>polygon_mask</code> takes a connected terrain field and outputs a mask for a closed polygon. Its <code>points</code> are at least three noncollinear <code>[x,z]</code> pairs in terrain-local world units. Each coordinate must fit the finite native float range. The graph editor uses the same structured point rows as Path Distance Mask. The last point connects to the first automatically; repeated points and an explicit closing point are valid.</p>
<p>The mask is 1 inside and on the boundary. <code>falloff</code> is a finite, nonnegative world-unit distance for a smooth transition outside the polygon; zero gives a hard edge. Either winding and concave polygons are accepted. Self-intersections use the even-odd fill rule: crossing an edge switches between inside and outside. Defaults are points <code>[[0,0],[100,0],[100,100],[0,100]]</code> and falloff 20.</p>
<p>For a basin, connect Noise to Height Blend's A input, a lower Flat terrain to B, and Polygon Mask to Mask. Use a higher flat height for a plateau. For an island, blend a low ocean-floor constant with elevated noise using the polygon mask. Set Height Blend's amount to 1 to follow the mask completely. The mask and blend nodes shape terrain; they do not publish water by themselves. The built-in Coastal Island preset also connects an Ocean Water node, with sea level zero and a shore biome.</p>
<h3>Comment nodes</h3>
<p>Drag <strong>Comment</strong> from <strong>Terrain Nodes → Notes</strong> onto a graph. Notes display wrapped text; expand <strong>Edit note</strong> to edit multiple lines and click outside to commit. Comments have stable node IDs and support move, copy/paste, duplicate, delete and graph Undo/Redo. They have no connection pins and cannot be a terrain output. Their text and position save with the graph but do not change generated terrain or its generation cache identity.</p>
<p>Built-in provenance uses <code>builtin://terrain/flat-building-site</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/desert-dunes</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/rolling-hills</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/mountain-range</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/basin</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/plateau</code>, <code>builtin://terrain/coastal-island</code> and <code>builtin://terrain/drainage-river</code>. Applied recipes contain their settings and graph in full; they do not require an external starter file. <code>demi terrain presets &lt;project&gt;</code> lists both built-in and project presets. The same IDs work with <code>demi terrain apply-preset</code>.</p>
<p>A landscape preset is a reusable, versioned data asset that configures the shared generator. The <strong>Terrain Presets</strong> tab beside Inspector and Terrain Nodes lists starter landscapes and imported project presets. It is available while a terrain graph is open; use <strong>View → Terrain Presets</strong> if hidden. Choose a preset and <strong>Apply and generate</strong>, then confirm replacement of the current generation settings and graph. Unapplied graph edits are replaced; compatible sculpt strokes and painted regions are retained. A grid change with existing edits is staged: press <strong>Generate</strong> in Terrain Graph to choose how to handle it. Applying is one undoable recipe edit; Undo and Redo move the recipe and visible surface together. The same operation is available from the command line as <code>demi terrain apply-preset</code>.</p>
<p><code>examples/terrain_3d</code> includes <strong>Mountain Range</strong>, <strong>River Valley</strong>, <strong>Rolling Hills</strong> and <strong>Coastal Island</strong> presets. Rolling Hills sets gentle relief; River Valley uses channel and gravel-bar landforms with riverbank biome rules; Coastal Island sets an ocean-floor landform and shoreline biomes around higher island ground. These are terrain-shape and biome settings, not authored or rendered water surfaces.</p>
<p>A preset is a data asset whose manifest declares <code>settings.content_type</code> as <code>terrain_palette</code>'s sibling <code>terrain_preset</code>, and it is referenced by a normal <code>asset://</code> URI. The document may supply any of <code>size</code>, <code>cells_x</code> and <code>cells_z</code> (the preset names resolution per axis, while the recipe it produces uses <code>resolution</code> as one pair), <code>chunk_cells</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>default_biome</code>, <code>biomes</code>, <code>layers</code> and <code>rules</code>, an optional <code>graph</code> using the normal versioned terrain graph format (including comment nodes), plus a required <code>name</code>, an optional <code>label</code> and <code>description</code> and a <code>format_version</code> of 1. Keys the preset omits fall back to canonical recipe defaults, so a preset that only sets a seed still produces a valid recipe. Rule objects inside a preset use exactly the same grammar and the same rejections as authored rules.</p>
<p>Applying a preset merges it into the author's recipe once and stamps <code>preset_id</code> and <code>preset_version</code>. Both keys are always recorded together; a half-stamped recipe is rejected, because a version without an id would make a recipe look applied when it is not. The recipe therefore remains self-describing: it regenerates identically from its own contents, and provenance is recorded rather than implied by a reference. Provenance is also part of the locality contract, so applying a different preset is treated as a real recipe change rather than a display preference.</p>
<p>Strokes are never templated. <code>regions</code>, <code>edits</code> and <code>exclusions</code> always come from the author's recipe, so applying a preset cannot destroy painting or sculpting. The merged document is then validated as a whole, which means your strokes are checked against the biomes they will actually be generated with, not against the ones the recipe had before.</p>
<p>That guarantee has a consequence worth knowing before you apply one to painted terrain. Because your strokes survive, a <code>regions</code> entry must name a biome the preset actually defines, and applying a preset whose biome set does not include your region names is refused with a clear error rather than quietly discarding the stroke. The fix is on your side: either pick a preset that defines those biome names, or rename the regions to match the preset. Renaming a region is safe, because a region stores the biome <em>name</em> and nothing else references it.</p>
<p>The reason a preset is applied rather than referenced is worth stating plainly: the recipe stays the single source of truth for generation. Incremental editing, the rebuild-versus-replay decision and undo/redo all operate on the recipe, so a runtime override would make each of those three answer a different question from the one the author asked. A preset applied once cannot also disagree with the terrain it was applied to.</p>

<h2>Asset palettes</h2>
<p>A <code>terrain_palette</code> DataAsset separates surface material bindings from object placement rules. Palettes use <code>format_version: 2</code>; manifests and material sets retain their own format versions.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 2,
  "name": "Meadow",
  "material_set": "asset://terrain/material_sets/meadow",
  "placements": {
    "pine_sparse": {
      "prefab": "prefab://vegetation/pine",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "spacing": 12,
      "scale": [0.9, 1.3],
      "biomes": ["meadow"],
      "collision": "static"
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>The IDs above illustrate the format; choose assets installed in your project. <code>material_set</code> is optional and references a <code>terrain_material_set</code>. Its surface bindings never produce scattered entities. <code>placements</code> is optional; a palette must supply a material set or at least one placement.</p>
<p>Each placement has an author-chosen stable ID using letters, digits, underscores or hyphens, and exactly one geometry reference: <code>model</code> to a Model3D asset, or <code>prefab</code> to an entity prefab. Prefabs own their materials and need no dummy asset reference. Multiple rules can reuse the same geometry with different settings. Renaming a rule changes its generated object identities.</p>
<p>Rules retain <code>weight</code> (default 1, zero disables selection), <code>spacing</code> (default 1, zero disables spacing rejection), <code>scale</code> (default [1,1]), <code>collision</code> (none/static/trigger, default static), <code>lod</code> metadata (default 0), and an optional <code>biomes</code> filter. These fields do not imply implemented vegetation LOD. Import/reimport records model and material-set dependencies; prefab source traversal owns prefab dependencies.</p>
<p>Use the palette editor's <strong>Surface Materials</strong> and <strong>Object Placements</strong> sections. Add a model or prefab placement, then edit its rule ID and settings. Rule edits, removal and material-set changes use normal Undo/Redo and Save.</p>
<p><strong>Migration:</strong> version-1 palettes are rejected explicitly. Move ground bindings into a material set; move object entries from <code>roles</code> to <code>placements</code>. Replace a model entry's <code>asset</code> with <code>model</code>; for prefab entries retain <code>prefab</code> and remove the dummy asset. Keep required surface roles on the material set. Reimport manifests and recook derived terrain payloads. The repository meadow palette is a migrated example.</p>
<h3>Automatic surface materials</h3>
<p>A recipe's palette can bind a material set without any object placements. Generate resolves the final terrain's slope, moisture, drainage and water coverage into surface roles. Each triangle uses its vertices' majority role; when all three differ, the first vertex determines the role. The result uses indexed sections of the existing terrain mesh, not proxy entities.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "name": "Landscape surfaces",
  "roles": {
    "ground": "asset://materials/soil",
    "rock": "asset://materials/rock",
    "underwater": "asset://materials/riverbed"
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Import this document as a <code>terrain_material_set</code> DataAsset and reference it from the palette's <code>material_set</code>. Bind ordinary <code>Material</code> assets. Available roles are <code>ground</code>, <code>grass</code>, <code>rock</code>, <code>cliff</code>, <code>sediment</code>, <code>sand</code>, <code>snow</code>, <code>wet_ground</code> and <code>underwater</code>. Typed <code>terrain_material</code> PBR definitions are not yet renderable by this path; Generate reports that limitation instead of pretending to render them.</p>
<p>An explicit biome <code>material</code> takes precedence. Biome colors continue to tint the material. Missing role bindings, or samples without a winning role, use the biome tint; the Terrain Graph panel reports these cases. Raised lakes and rivers use resolved finite-body coverage rather than a global sea plane. The <code>terrain_graph_3d</code> example supplies all nine surface bindings without scatter objects.</p>
<p>Changing only material-set bindings retains heights, collision geometry and classification. Sculpting recomputes classification, including effects from nonlocal drainage, while publishing only changed surface groups. Save and cooked payloads preserve the same classification and bindings. Reimport changed source assets and regenerate the preview to apply them.</p>
<p><strong>Rendering boundary:</strong> this is discrete material assignment. Smooth blending, triplanar projection, terrain PBR texture maps and vegetation instancing remain separate work. Existing authored document versions remain unchanged; derived terrain caches must be regenerated and shipped projects recooked.</p>
<h3>Scattering a palette onto terrain</h3>
<p>Name the palette on the recipe with a <code>palette</code> field holding an <code>asset://</code> id. A recipe without one scatters nothing, so the feature is opt-in. In the conventional pipeline, a palette change takes the full generation path rather than a local brush replay. In a terrain graph, a palette change invalidates the Scatter Palette branch and its downstream output; unchanged upstream nodes can be reused from a previous graph result.</p>
<p>In the conventional pipeline, scattering reads the finished surface after sculpting and uses the final biome, water and exclusion constraints. In a graph, Scatter Palette reads the heightfield connected to its input before the final authored region, sculpt and exclusion overlays. After graph output, placements snap to edited heights and placements on excluded samples are removed. A newly painted biome region therefore does not guarantee that graph scatter will choose different biome-specific roles; connect a Biome Rules node upstream when rule-based biomes should inform candidate selection.</p>
<p><code>weight</code> is a relative share of eligible ground, so weight 0 keeps a role available but never selects it. <code>spacing</code> is a minimum distance enforced per role, so one role's spacing never rejects another's, and <code>spacing</code> of 0 is continuous, unconstrained cover. The resulting placements are recorded against the terrain, and reaching the placement ceiling is reported rather than silently accepted.</p>
<p>Placement is deterministic in the same way generation is: a candidate draws from the scatter sub-seed combined with its own cell and role, so the result does not depend on the order cells are visited, on how many workers ran, or on how many times the terrain is scattered.</p>
<p>Placements become real objects. When a scene loads and after every terrain update, each placement is reconciled into an object under its terrain owner, and a placement is identified by the cell that owns it rather than by its position. A stroke that moves the ground under an object moves that object instead of destroying and rebuilding it, so per-instance state survives. Placements the new terrain no longer produces are removed, and two placement rules landing in one cell stay distinct.</p>
<p>The <code>examples/terrain_3d</code> palette uses a clearly labelled cube prefab for its six vegetation/prop rules. Soil, sand, snow, wet ground and exposed-rock surface bindings live in its material set and create no proxy entities. Replace the remaining prototype prefab with role-specific art for a game. Failed prefab composition prevents the scene from becoming ready.</p>

<h2>Inspecting terrain from the command line</h2>
<pre><code>demi terrain inspect &lt;recipe.json&gt; [--format text|json]
demi terrain explain &lt;recipe.json&gt; --at &lt;x&gt;,&lt;z&gt; [--format text|json]
demi terrain seeds &lt;seed&gt; [--format text|json]</code></pre>
<p>These commands accept a recipe JSON document: the <code>recipe</code> object from a Terrain asset source or an explicit inline Terrain3D recipe. They do not accept the enclosing scene or Terrain asset document. <code>inspect</code> reads the recipe without generating a field, and reports size, resolution, sample count, seed, chunk size, default biome, derived sea level, the biome table, the layer tree, every rule with its conditions, stroke counts by kind and the derived sub-seeds.</p>

<p>Two more verbs apply a preset:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>demi terrain presets &lt;project&gt;</code> lists every <code>terrain_preset</code> data asset in the project with its name, description and a summary of its biomes and rules. A project with no presets prints an empty result and still succeeds.</li>
  <li><code>demi terrain apply-preset &lt;project&gt; --preset &lt;asset://id&gt;</code> currently targets an inline Terrain3D recipe in the scene, writing it through the shared small-diff patcher. If the scene has more than one terrain, pass <code>--entity &lt;id&gt;</code>. For an asset-backed terrain, apply presets in the Terrain Graph; this CLI command does not yet follow the asset reference.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your work survives either path: applying a preset keeps every <code>regions</code>, <code>edits</code> and <code>exclusions</code> entry. A preset that changes the terrain size or resolution is not applied blindly. With protected areas present the engine refuses it and says why, and otherwise it asks whether to keep your radial edits before generating at the new size.</p>
<p><code>explain</code> answers the question a rule list cannot: why is this point this biome? It generates the field, then reports the assigned biome and where the assignment came from: a named rule, a painted region override, or the default biome. It prints the full rule context for that sample, and then either the conditions of the winning rule or, when nothing matched, every active rule with each band marked <code>INSIDE</code> or <code>OUTSIDE</code> against the sample value and the first condition that rejected it. Progress and diagnostics go to stderr, so stdout stays parseable for <code>--format json</code>.</p>
<pre><code>$ demi terrain explain recipe.json --at 10,110
Position: 10.000,110.000 in terrain 128.000 x 128.000
Cell: 10,110 (sample 14200 of 16641)
Sea level: 8.500

Biome: sand
Source: rule "shoreline"

Context:
  height          0.274
  slope           6.638 deg
  moisture        0.268
  water distance  0.000
  substrate       wet

Rule "shoreline" biome=sand priority=30 blend=1.500
      slope          unconstrained
      moisture       unconstrained
      water distance [0.000,6.000] sample 0.000 -&gt; INSIDE
      elevation      unconstrained
      substrate      unconstrained</code></pre>
<p>The tool reports band containment facts and takes the assignment from the runtime decision. It does not reimplement the runtime's blending weighting, so a <code>blend</code> ramp is visible as a matched band rather than as a fractional score. That is a deliberate limit: it can tell you a rule did or did not accept a sample, and it will not invent a number the runtime never produces.</p>
<p><code>seeds</code> prints the derived sub-seed of every seed channel for a world seed. The channels are <code>landform</code>, <code>landform_warp</code>, <code>erosion</code>, <code>hydrology</code>, <code>biome_placement</code> and <code>scatter</code>. The separate warp channel keeps coordinate displacement independent of the elevation-noise sequence; disabling warp preserves the original landform sampling. Erosion, drainage and water processing, and scattering are implemented stages; which ones run depends on the recipe or connected graph nodes. The command reports stable channel values even when a particular terrain does not use a stage. Each sub-seed is derived by hashing the channel's <em>name</em> rather than its position in the enum, so adding or reordering a channel cannot change an existing channel's value.</p>
<p>The generator version is folded into the terrain generation cache key, alongside the canonical recipe. Graph node positions are excluded from that key; changing connections or parameters changes it. A resolved palette and its referenced asset hashes are included, so changing palette content cannot serve placements from an older input.</p>

<h2>Changing terrain dimensions</h2>
<p>If size or resolution changes while edits exist, the editor asks how to proceed. Radial edits can keep their terrain-local positions. Protection snapshots require the original grid: keep that grid or explicitly clear the edits before generating a resized terrain. Clearing is undoable. Cancelling generation or a failed generation leaves the previously committed terrain intact.</p>

<h2>Saved source and generated geometry</h2>
<p>A Terrain asset stores a versioned recipe, region strokes and manual edits; a scene placement stores its asset reference. Neither stores generated triangle lists or one authored entity per chunk. Generated child meshes and colliders exist in the loaded world. Explicit inline procedural recipes remain available in scenes and prefabs.</p>
<p>Development tools prepare uncached source assets; shipped games load cooked heightfields without rerunning generation. Chunk geometry and native resources are built from the loaded field. Background work covers generation, but scene publication, mesh construction and GPU/physics updates can still take time on large terrains. Render LOD selects detail per camera using the terrain owner's pixel-error setting. It does not implement large-world streaming.</p>

<h2>Try the example</h2>
<pre><code>demi editor --project examples/terrain_3d
demi run --project examples/terrain_3d</code></pre>
<p>Select <strong>Paint and sculpt this terrain</strong> in the hierarchy. The example has meadow, mountain and sand biomes, plus a flattened building site. Change the seed and regenerate: the flatten edit remains and the building stays in the scene.</p>
<p>Noise generation uses <a href="https://github.com/Auburn/FastNoiseLite">FastNoiseLite</a> under its MIT license. The dependency is pinned in the engine build configuration.</p>
